  "name": "kernel-splice.musa",
  "kind": "piece",
  "source": "// A kernel quote is the one place Musa lets a composer write the assembly the\n// compiler normally writes for them. `kernel EventTrack[WrittenTime, ScoreFact] { ... }` is a\n// composition expression of the temporal kernel — the same grammar a\n// `.musa.kernel` file is written in — and `${...}` splices ordinary Musa music\n// into it. What it buys is placement no surface constructor spells; what it\n// costs is stated at the bottom of this file.\npiece \"Kernel Splice\" {\n    meter 4/4;\n\n    // Ordinary music, checked the ordinary way. Nothing about it knows it is\n    // going to be quoted.\n    let subject: Music = music {\n        c4/4\n        d4/4\n        e4/4\n        f4/4\n    };\n\n    // One term, assembled by hand. `${subject}` is a hole: it is instantiated\n    // once, here, under this piece's context, and bound to a fresh name the\n    // compiler chooses — so nothing written inside the quote can capture it,\n    // whatever the quote happens to call its own bindings.\n    //\n    // `subj` is referenced three times and elaborated once. That is sharing, and\n    // it is why the three references below are three placements of the *same*\n    // facts rather than three re-readings of `subject`.\n    let assembled: Music = kernel EventTrack[WrittenTime, ScoreFact] {\n        let subj = ${subject} in\n        together {\n            subj;\n            // Diminution, a measure later. The halving is written in the\n            // host, not as a raw `scale by`: renotating is a payload\n            // operation and the kernel never touches payloads, so raw scaling\n            // would move these notes without changing what is written on\n            // them. A second hole is a second instantiation, and gets a locus\n            // of its own.\n            shift by 1 ${stretch(1/2, subject)};\n            // Observation: the fourth note alone, read out of the material\n            // through a window and placed at the start of the last measure.\n            // Restricting keeps the extent, so this branch still runs to 2.\n            shift by 1 restrict from 3/4 to 1 subj;\n            // Raw material, written as facts. A payload states what the\n            // material *is*; it states no scope and no origin, because the\n            // use decides which voice this lands in and the quote is where it\n            // came from. Writing either is refused.\n            track 2 {\n                occurrence \"note c2 1\" from 0 to 1;\n                occurrence \"note g2 1\" from 1 to 2;\n            };\n        }\n    };\n\n    score {\n        part ensemble {\n            voice line {\n                // The guarantees the quote keeps are exact time and payload\n                // typing: the term's extent is 2 whole notes, computed from\n                // its structure, and every payload in it is a `ScoreFact`.\n                //\n                // The guarantees it does *not* keep are the theory ones, and\n                // an assertion around the completed quote is how they come\n                // back. This claim is checked against the assembled facts, so\n                // it sees the raw notes and the spliced ones alike.\n                assert pitches_in(scale c major) {\n                    use assembled;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // What a quote gives up, precisely:\n    //\n    // A hole is instantiated once and the facts it produced are frozen from\n    // then on. A raw `shift`, `scale` or `restrict` around a reference moves\n    // facts that already exist; it does not re-elaborate the hole under the\n    // context the reference sits in. So an assertion that passed *inside*\n    // `subject` — that it fills a measure, say — says nothing about the\n    // diminished copy above, which fills half of one. Bar alignment, scale\n    // membership and chord realization are properties of where facts land,\n    // and raw placement is exactly the freedom to land them elsewhere.\n    //\n    // Raw `shift`, `scale` and `restrict` are operations on *time*. `shift`\n    // and `restrict` want nothing of the payloads and are safe to write here;\n    // `scale` halves every span and leaves every written value alone, which\n    // is why the diminution above is spelled `${stretch(1/2, subject)}` and\n    // not `scale by 1/2`. Raw scaling is for material whose written values\n    // already say what was meant.\n    //\n    // Every fact that leaves a quote records a `splice` step in its expansion\n    // path, at the locus it was assembled at, so Origin can say that a note\n    // with no surface statement behind it came from here.\n}\n",
